WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:12.120
Photos, videos, maps, navigation apps, location history, Wi-Fi context, Bluetooth context, cell network context, and sensor-related application data can all contribute to a mobile investigation.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.380
These artifacts can help place a device, account, or application in a broader timeline.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.680
They can also be misunderstood if treated as exact, complete, or always tied to user intent.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:30.700
Media files may contain EXIF metadata, depending on how the file was created, edited, transmitted, or stored.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:37.160
EXIF can include camera information, timestamps, device information, and sometimes geotags.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:44.820
But metadata can be absent, stripped, altered by applications, copied from another device, or affected by time settings.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.840
EXIF is evidence to evaluate, not a guarantee of authorship or presence.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:57.820
Maps and navigation apps may store searches, destinations, cached map data, routes, or application state.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:04.200
Location history may exist locally, in app data, or in cloud services depending on settings and account behavior.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:15.540
The interpretation depends on what generated the record: a foreground navigation session, background location service, synced account data, an app cache, or a server-side service.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:21.360
Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and cell-related context can help explain environment and movement at a high level.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:30.060
A device may record known networks, nearby devices, connection history, or carrier-related context depending on artifact availability.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.440
These sources are usually better at supporting possibilities and timelines than proving precise physical location by themselves.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.300
Timestamp normalization is essential.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:48.800
Android artifacts may use Unix epoch values, milliseconds, local time, UTC, application-specific formats, or provider-specific records.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.460
Time zones and daylight saving changes can affect interpretation.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:02:05.400
Location evidence should be corroborated and reported with uncertainty because artifacts can be approximate, incomplete, synced, stale, or generated by application behavior rather than direct user action.
